data platform_ingestion_note: String = "READ, NOT RECALLED. The v1.00 PDF at the subject module's public CDN locator was fetched and its text extracted on 2026-08-14 — content streams inflated and each string decoded through the PDF's own per-font ToUnicode CMap — so every row below is transcribed from a section this repository actually read, and the section is named. What is deliberately NOT carried yet: tables, figures, layout geometry, impedance targets and electrical limits — an ingestion-scope boundary, not a carriage refusal. The document is footed 'Ampere Computing Proprietary' on every page; that marking is a recorded attribute that decides nothing (gunbc.upstream_document_carriage, extdeps.cpu.ampere_altra_package). This module takes structural statements from running text only; the same public-availability axis that permits the rows below bars Customer Connect collateral, not the footer."

// THE REVISION THIS REPOSITORY READ IS SUPERSEDED, AND THE STANDING IS THE
// SUBJECT MODULE'S FACT, NOT A SECOND ONE HERE. The subject carries
// HeldSpecificationSupersessionStanding with the newer revision label, its date
// and the index authority; this module PROJECTS that standing rather than
// re-declaring the version and date, so the two cannot drift apart (review
// 51969). Ampere's index lists revision 1.20 dated 2025-07-15 while the copy
// reachable without a Customer Connect login is v1.00, so the answer below is
// false — a citation pinned to a superseded revision that does not say so is
// the stale-citation class, and the newer revision's facts are not guessed at,
// summarised or assumed unchanged.
fn platform_facts_are_from_the_latest_revision() -> Bool {
  match altra_platform_hw_design_subject.supersession_standing {
    HeldRevisionIsCurrentPublicCopy => true
    NewerRevisionExistsUnretrieved { newer_version_label, newer_publication_date, index_authority, held_version_label, detail } => false
  }
}

data altra_platform_memory_generation: extdeps.memory.types.DramGeneration = dram_generation_ddr4

// MEMORY TOPOLOGY (section 2.2.1). The specification admits exactly two linear
// topologies. A population outside them is not a smaller version of a supported
// one, so the admitted set is a closed coproduct rather than a DIMM count.
type DimmsPerChannelTopology
  = OneDimmPerChannel
  | TwoDimmsPerChannel

fn dimms_per_channel_count(topology: DimmsPerChannelTopology) -> Int {
  match topology {
    OneDimmPerChannel => 1
    TwoDimmsPerChannel => 2
  }
}

// Derived from the package authority's channel count, never a second literal.
fn dimm_slots_per_socket(topology: DimmsPerChannelTopology) -> Int {
  altra_ddr4_channel_count * dimms_per_channel_count(topology: topology)
}

fn altra_platform_max_dimm_slots_per_socket() -> Int {
  dimm_slots_per_socket(topology: TwoDimmsPerChannel)
}

fn altra_platform_package_dimm_ceiling() -> Int {
  altra_max_dimms_per_socket
}

// Section 2.2.3, and it is a REQUIREMENT on slot assignment rather than a note
// about a figure: with 2DPC, Slot0 (DIMM0) is the slot farther from the CPU and
// Slot1 (DIMM1) is closer, which is what decides where a 1DPC population goes.
type DimmSlotPosition = DimmSlot0 | DimmSlot1

fn dimm_slot_farther_from_cpu() -> DimmSlotPosition {
  DimmSlot0
}

fn one_dimm_per_channel_populated_slot() -> DimmSlotPosition {
  DimmSlot0
}

// PCIe / CCIX SUBSYSTEM (section 2.3.1). Eight 16-lane multi-port root
// complexes, four of each type; the lane total is DERIVED from that structure
// and joined against the package authority's declared Gen4 lane count, so the
// two Ampere documents disagreeing surfaces as a failing join.
type RootComplexType = RootComplexTypeA | RootComplexTypeB

data altra_root_complex_lane_width: Int = 16

data altra_type_a_root_complex_count: Int = 4

data altra_type_b_root_complex_count: Int = 4

data altra_pcie_controller_count: Int = 48

fn altra_root_complex_count() -> Int {
  altra_type_a_root_complex_count + altra_type_b_root_complex_count
}

fn altra_derived_lane_total() -> Int {
  altra_root_complex_count() * altra_root_complex_lane_width
}

fn altra_declared_lane_total() -> Int {
  altra_pcie_gen4_lane_count
}

fn root_complex_count_of_type(kind: RootComplexType) -> Int {
  match kind {
    RootComplexTypeA => altra_type_a_root_complex_count
    RootComplexTypeB => altra_type_b_root_complex_count
  }
}

// A Type A root complex carries a dual-mode x16 PCIe/CCIX controller and a Type
// B root complex does not, so CCIX capability is a property of the TYPE rather
// than a count standing beside it.
fn root_complex_supports_ccix(kind: RootComplexType) -> Bool {
  match kind {
    RootComplexTypeA => true
    RootComplexTypeB => false
  }
}

// The admitted SerDes groupings, from section 2.3.1. Each grouping partitions
// its root complex's lanes, which is the property a witness can check: a
// fabricated grouping fails the lane sum instead of merely reading plausibly.
type TypeALaneGrouping
  = TypeAOneX16
  | TypeATwoX8
  | TypeAOneX8AndTwoX4
  | TypeAFourX4

fn type_a_grouping_lane_total(grouping: TypeALaneGrouping) -> Int {
  match grouping {
    TypeAOneX16 => 16
    TypeATwoX8 => 8 + 8
    TypeAOneX8AndTwoX4 => 8 + 4 + 4
    TypeAFourX4 => 4 + 4 + 4 + 4
  }
}

// A Type B root complex's x16 divides into two identical x8 halves, so a Type B
// grouping is a property of a HALF and its lane sum is 8, not 16.
type TypeBHalfGrouping
  = TypeBOneX8
  | TypeBTwoX4

data altra_type_b_half_lane_width: Int = 8

fn type_b_half_grouping_lane_total(grouping: TypeBHalfGrouping) -> Int {
  match grouping {
    TypeBOneX8 => 8
    TypeBTwoX4 => 4 + 4
  }
}

data altra_ccix_x16_links_per_socket: Int = 2

data altra_pcie_only_lane_count: Int = 96

data altra_ccix_link_framing_note: String = "TWO STATEMENTS IN ONE DOCUMENT, CARRIED AS THEY STAND. Section 2.3.1 says Altra provides 128 PCIe Gen4 lanes with 48 controllers to support 96 lanes of PCIe and up to 2 x16 Cache Coherent Interconnect for Accelerators links. The overview, section 1.4, instead lists 4 CCIX links beside 192 lanes in 2P configuration. The two are NOT reconciled here and no third number is derived from them: the section 2.3.1 figures are per socket at a stated split, the overview bullets are family-level and their grain is not stated, and inventing a reconciliation would be the fabricated-plausible-output failure DESIGN section 5 forbids. Consumers take the per-socket rows; a grain the document itself states is the only thing that retires this note."

data altra_overview_ccix_link_count: Int = 4

data altra_overview_two_socket_lane_count: Int = 192

// DUAL-SOCKET INTERCONNECT (section 2.1, running text): a dual-socket
// implementation uses TWO x16 CCIX ports. The signalling rate printed inside
// the figure is deliberately not carried: figure content is outside the
// running-text boundary this module holds while the proprietary-marking
// question is open, and a rate is exactly the kind of number that boundary is
// drawn around.
data altra_two_socket_ccix_lane_width: Int = 16

data altra_two_socket_ccix_port_count: Int = 2

// REFERENCE CLOCKS (section 2.3.2). Two defined refclk inputs, both 100 MHz
// PCIe-compliant differential HCSL inputs. REFCLK_SRNS 'is always needed and
// must always be provided'; REFCLK_SRIS is the spread-spectrum refclk for the
// PCIe SerDes only. The requirement is therefore derived from the input rather
// than being a flag a board designer may set either way.
type AltraRefclkInput = RefclkSrns | RefclkSris

data altra_refclk_frequency: std.measure.Hertz = hertz(count: 100000000)

fn refclk_always_required(input: AltraRefclkInput) -> Bool {
  match input {
    RefclkSrns => true
    RefclkSris => false
  }
}

// PCIe RESET (section 2.3.3). Altra has 48 PCIe controllers and does NOT have a
// dedicated PERST for each; the platform must decode reset requests in a CPLD
// driven by at most six GPIO outputs. Modelled as a closed scheme so the
// absence of per-controller PERST is a structural constraint on any board built
// to this document rather than an omission a designer may assume away.
type AltraPcieResetScheme
  = CpldDecodedPerst { gpio_output_max: Int }

data altra_pcie_reset_scheme: AltraPcieResetScheme = CpldDecodedPerst {
  gpio_output_max: 6,
}

fn altra_has_dedicated_perst_per_controller() -> Bool {
  false
}

// HOT PLUG (section 2.3.5): no native hot-plug controller, so hot plug is an
// external controller reached over I2C or GPIO depending on the platform.
type AltraHotPlugRealization
  = ExternalHotPlugControllerOverI2c
  | ExternalHotPlugControllerOverGpio

fn altra_has_native_hot_plug_controller() -> Bool {
  false
}

// WHAT THIS DOCUMENT DOES NOT SAY, MODELLED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE REDISCOVERED.
// A reader looking for attachment geometry will otherwise re-read the whole
// specification to learn that the answer is not in it, which is a cost paid
// once per reader. Each mechanical fact a board needs is classified by WHERE
// the document puts it, and the three states are genuinely different: a fact in
// running text is one this repository can read and quote, a fact inside a
// figure is drawn geometry it cannot read at all (the drawing's text is
// rendered from embedded subset fonts with no character mapping, so guessing at
// it is exactly the fabricated-plausible-output failure), and an absent fact is
// one no ruling and no access can produce from this document.
type MechanicalFactAvailability
  = StatedInRunningText { section: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | StatedOnlyInsideFigure { figure: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | AbsentFromDocument

// THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN PUBLICATION STATUS, carried here beside its other facts
// rather than in a shared file: whether a document is publicly published is a
// fact about that document. The RULE that turns a status into what this
// repository may carry is gunbc policy and lives in
// gunbc.upstream_document_carriage; nothing in this module decides it.
data altra_platform_hw_design_publication_status: extdeps.publication.DocumentPublicationStatus = extdeps.publication.PubliclyPublishedWithRestrictiveMarking {
  marking: "Ampere Computing Proprietary",
}

// CARRIAGE DERIVES FROM PUBLIC AVAILABILITY, NOT FROM THE MARKING (operator
// ruling, 2026-08-14). This document is published publicly and is footed
// "Ampere Computing Proprietary" on every page; the footer is a recorded
// attribute that decides nothing, and the facts below are carried because the
// document is public. The Customer Connect board collateral remains barred on
// the SAME axis rather than a different one: it is not publicly published. The
// distinction from an OCP contribution survives and is not flattened away —
// Mt. Mitchell carries an explicit open licence (OWFa 1.0 plus the CLA), this
// one is public-but-proprietary, and those are the same permission arrived at
// differently.

data altra_platform_keepout_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = AbsentFromDocument

data altra_platform_coordinate_origin_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = AbsentFromDocument

data altra_platform_ddr_routing_rule_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = AbsentFromDocument

data altra_platform_package_outline_drawing_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = StatedOnlyInsideFigure {
  figure: "Figure 3",
}

data altra_platform_orientation_datum_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = StatedOnlyInsideFigure {
  figure: "Figure 3",
}

data altra_platform_pad_array_geometry_availability: MechanicalFactAvailability = StatedInRunningText {
  section: "1.9",
}

// SECTION 1.9, PACKAGE OVERVIEW, IN RUNNING TEXT. Micrometres keep every figure
// exact: 77.080 mm, 67.000 mm and the 0.87 / 0.5 mm hexagonal pitch pair are
// all sub-millimetre-grained.
//
// WHAT THIS DOCUMENT INDEPENDENTLY STATES, JOINED TO THE PACKAGE AUTHORITY RATHER THAN ALIASED TO
// IT. The canonical facts are the package's -- extdeps.cpu.ampere_altra_package owns the body
// extent and the land pitch, because they are properties of the part. These rows are this
// specification's own reading of the same quantities, and they stay independent values on purpose:
// an alias would make agreement true by construction and therefore unable to express disagreement,
// which is the one thing corroboration is for. If a future revision of this document states
// 77.081 mm, that has to become visible rather than silently inheriting the datasheet's number.
// The join is asserted by an executing witness, which is what makes these rows corroboration
// instead of the two unjoined literals that were here before -- the defect was never the second
// number, it was that nothing compared them. This is the same shape altra_pad_count already used
// against the package's contact count.
data altra_package_outline_width: std.measure.Micrometer = micrometer(count: 77080)

data altra_package_outline_depth: std.measure.Micrometer = micrometer(count: 67000)

data altra_pad_pitch_dx: std.measure.Micrometer = micrometer(count: 870)

data altra_pad_pitch_dy: std.measure.Micrometer = micrometer(count: 500)

data altra_pad_count: Int = 4926

data altra_pad_column_count: Int = 84

data altra_pad_row_count: Int = 126

// The pad population this document states, joined against the package
// authority's socket row, so the platform specification and the datasheet
// disagreeing surfaces as a failing join instead of two literals drifting.
fn altra_platform_declared_pad_count() -> Int {
  altra_pad_count
}

fn altra_package_declared_contact_count() -> Int {
  altra_declared_contact_count()
}

// Nx times Ny is the bounding grid, NOT a pad count, and the gap between them
// is NOT interpretable as the depopulated region from text alone: the array is
// hexagonal (the document gives a Dx/Dy pair rather than one pitch), so a
// staggered row does not occupy every column position, and Figure 4's
// depopulated ball region is a second, independent reason positions are empty.
// The document separates neither effect in text, so this module reports only
// that the grid exceeds the population and refuses to attribute the difference.
fn altra_pad_grid_position_count() -> Int {
  altra_pad_column_count * altra_pad_row_count
}

fn pad_grid_exceeds_pad_population() -> Bool {
  altra_pad_grid_position_count() > altra_pad_count
}

data altra_pcb_installation_note: String = "Section 1.9 states PCB installation: Using socket. That is a requirement on the board, not a description of the package: an Altra board attaches the processor through a socket, so the land pattern a board needs is the SOCKET's, and the package outline drawing and the socket drawing are separate references (both Figure 3). The datasheet names the designation ILM4926 in its thermal notes, and that designation is deliberately not re-declared here — it is named by two independent documents and belongs to neither of their modules."

// AN AVAILABLE FACT AND A POSITIONABLE ONE ARE DIFFERENT THINGS, and this is
// the row that keeps a permissive ruling from being read as more than it is:
// even with every section 1.9 number carried, the array could be sized and
// counted and still not placed, because no coordinate origin is stated in text
// anywhere in this document. Pin A1 is an orientation datum with no origin
// attached to it.
fn pad_array_is_positionable_from_running_text() -> Bool {
  match altra_platform_coordinate_origin_availability {
    StatedInRunningText { section } => true
    StatedOnlyInsideFigure { figure } => false
    AbsentFromDocument => false
  }
}

// SECTION 10, THE DOCUMENT'S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY. Carried because it is what
// closes the search: whether a further Ampere document exists to chase for
// package mechanics is DERIVED from this roster rather than asserted, so a
// later revision that adds a mechanical document changes the answer by changing
// the rows.
data altra_platform_bibliography: List<std.types.NonEmptyStr> = [
  "Altra Architecture Specifications",
  "Altra Debug Architecture Specifications",
  "Altra I/O List",
  "Altra Clocking Overview",
  "Altra Power Sequencing",
  "Altra Reset Specifications",
  "Altra TSM Design Specifications",
  "Altra BMC Interface Specifications",
  "Altra Software Overview",
]

fn bibliography_entry_is_package_mechanical(title: std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  string_contains(s: title as String, pattern: "Mechanical")
    || string_contains(s: title as String, pattern: "Package")
    || string_contains(s: title as String, pattern: "Socket")
    || string_contains(s: title as String, pattern: "Land")
}

fn bibliography_lists_a_package_mechanical_document() -> Bool {
  any(altra_platform_bibliography, t => bibliography_entry_is_package_mechanical(title: t))
}

// THE BIBLIOGRAPHY IS NOT THE DOCUMENT'S REFERENCE SET, and reading it as one is what made the
// row above answer a question it could not see. Section 10 lists nine titles and none is a layout
// or mechanical document, so "is there a further Ampere document to chase" derived false. It is
// false about section 10 and false as an answer: sections 4.2.2 and 4.3.2 each say, in running
// text, to refer to a layout document that section 10 never lists.
//
// That is the empty-observation narrow in miniature — an observation that could not express a
// referenced document rendered as the verdict "no referenced document exists", so the search
// closed instead of widening. The remedy is a second roster over a different part of the
// document, not a wider match on the first: the two populations have different extents and
// fusing them would put a title in a bibliography that does not contain it.
//
// EACH ENTRY CARRIES ITS OWN REACHABILITY, because that is the whole reason to carry them.
// A referenced document this repository can fetch and one behind a login are different
// obligations with different owners, and a roster of bare titles cannot tell them apart.
type ReferencedDocumentReach
  = ReferencedDocumentPublic
  | ReferencedDocumentAccessGated { gate: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

type PlatformReferencedDocument {
  title: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  cited_by_section: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  reach: ReferencedDocumentReach
}

data altra_platform_referenced_documents: List<PlatformReferencedDocument> = [
  PlatformReferencedDocument {
    title: "Altra PCB Layout Guidelines",
    cited_by_section: "4.2.2 and 4.3.2, both titled Reference Layout and both sitting under VRD Implementation using Texas Instruments Devices (4.2) and VRD Implementation using Infineon Devices (4.3) — so the sections that name this document are about voltage-regulator layout",
    reach: ReferencedDocumentAccessGated { gate: "Ampere Customer Connect" },
  },
  PlatformReferencedDocument {
    title: "Altra PCIe Hot-Plug Specification",
    cited_by_section: "2.3.5",
    reach: ReferencedDocumentAccessGated { gate: "Ampere Customer Connect" },
  },
]

fn referenced_document_is_board_layout(d: PlatformReferencedDocument) -> Bool {
  string_contains(s: d.title as String, pattern: "Layout")
}

// THE ANSWER THE LANE ACTUALLY WANTS: is a layout document named anywhere in this specification,
// and if so can we reach it. Both halves, because a named unreachable document and no named
// document at all send people to opposite places.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT ANSWER, and the distinction is the whole point: a document being NAMED here is
// not that document being the authority for any particular layout. The one it names is cited from
// voltage-regulator sections (see cited_by_section), so this predicate says a layout document
// exists and is gated — never that the socket land pattern is inside it.
fn names_a_board_layout_document() -> Bool {
  any(altra_platform_referenced_documents, d => referenced_document_is_board_layout(d: d))
}

fn board_layout_document_is_reachable() -> Bool {
  any(altra_platform_referenced_documents, d =>
    referenced_document_is_board_layout(d: d)
      && match d.reach {
        ReferencedDocumentPublic => true
        ReferencedDocumentAccessGated { gate: _ } => false
      })
}

data altra_platform_impedance_scope_note: String = "WHAT THIS DOCUMENT'S IMPEDANCE TARGETS ARE NOT. Section 4.1.2 is 'Decoupling Requirements to Achieve Target Impedance' and section 4.4 is 'Power Integrity Simulations': every impedance figure in this specification is POWER-DELIVERY network target impedance, per rail, reached by decoupling. None of it is DDR trace impedance, and this document states no DDR routing rules, no trace impedance target, no length matching and no stackup. The layout guidance it does carry is power (4.1.1 Physical Design, 4.2.2 and 4.3.2 vendor reference layouts) and I2C (7.1.4). A downstream consumer reaching for a number from section 4 as a signal-integrity target would get a category error with plausible numbers attached, which is why the boundary is stated here rather than left to be inferred from what is absent."

data altra_platform_section_coverage_note: String = "WHAT THIS MODULE MODELS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT, so absence is never read as a claim about the document. MODELLED, from running text: the memory topologies and slot ordering (2.2), the root-complex structure and admitted lane groupings (2.3.1), the reference-clock inputs (2.3.2), the PCIe reset scheme (2.3.3), hot-plug realization (2.3.5) and the dual-socket CCIX interconnect (2.1). NOT MODELLED, each a real section rather than an oversight: the BMC subsystem (3), power supply design, decoupling, regulator selection and power sequencing (4), clock distribution (5), the PERST encode table (6.1), miscellaneous I/O interfaces (7), system boot sequences (8) and the hardware bring-up guide (9). Those are tables, figures and electrical limits not yet transcribed; carriage is permitted because the document is publicly published (altra_platform_hw_design_publication_status), and a consumer that needs one is what earns its transcription. CORRECTED BY A COMPLETE READ ON 2026-08-15: an earlier revision of this sentence said the socket land pattern was in that second group of not-yet-transcribed sections. It is not in this document at all, in any section, in text or in a figure — the document instead names an access-gated layout document (altra_platform_referenced_documents), and that naming is WEAKER than it first reads: both citing sections are vendor VRD reference layouts, so what the pointer routes to is voltage-regulator layout and whether the gated document also carries the socket land pattern is unverified rather than known. Corrected again on 2026-08-16 after extracting this document's own table of contents, because the previous wording called the gated document the authority for the land pattern, which conflates a document being named with a document being read. Keepout, DDR4 routing rules, impedance targets and stackup guidance remain genuinely untranscribed here, and some may exist only in the unretrieved 1.20 revision; the land pattern is a different case and must not be filed with them, because waiting for a transcription that can never arrive is indistinguishable from progress."
